<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-QR-factorization">
  <title><m>QR</m> factorization and least squares</title>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-qr-definition">
    <title>From Gram–Schmidt to <m>QR</m></title>

    <p>
      The <xref ref="alg-u4-gram-schmidt">Gram–Schmidt algorithm</xref> processes the independent columns of a matrix from left to right. It constructs orthonormal directions <m>\mathbf{q}_1,\ldots,\mathbf{q}_n</m> and records coefficients <m>r_{ij}</m>. These same quantities form a matrix factorization.
    </p>
    <p>
      Let
      <me>
        A
        =
        \begin{bmatrix}
        \mathbf{a}_1\amp\mathbf{a}_2\amp\cdots\amp\mathbf{a}_n
        \end{bmatrix}.
      </me>
    </p>
    <p>
      At column <m>j</m>, the <xref ref="alg-u4-gram-schmidt">Gram–Schmidt algorithm</xref> computes
      <me>
        \mathbf{p}_j
        =
        \sum_{i\lt j}
        r_{ij}\mathbf{q}_i,
        \qquad
        \mathbf{v}_j
        =
        \mathbf{a}_j-\mathbf{p}_j,
      </me>
      <me>
        r_{jj}
        =
        \|\mathbf{v}_j\|,
        \qquad
        \mathbf{q}_j
        =
        \frac{\mathbf{v}_j}{r_{jj}}.
      </me>
    </p>
    <p>
      Since
      <me>
        \mathbf{v}_j
        =
        r_{jj}\mathbf{q}_j,
      </me>
      we can reconstruct the original column:
      <me>
        \mathbf{a}_j
        =
        \mathbf{p}_j+\mathbf{v}_j
        =
        \sum_{i\lt j}
        r_{ij}\mathbf{q}_i
        +
        r_{jj}\mathbf{q}_j
        =
        \sum_{i\leq j}
        r_{ij}\mathbf{q}_i.
      </me>
    </p>
    <p>
      Thus column <m>j</m> uses only <m>\mathbf{q}_1,\ldots,\mathbf{q}_j</m>.
    </p>
    <p>
      Collect the orthonormal directions in
      <me>
        Q
        =
        \begin{bmatrix}
        \mathbf{q}_1\amp\mathbf{q}_2\amp\cdots\amp\mathbf{q}_n
        \end{bmatrix}
      </me>
      and the coefficients in
      <me>
        R
        =
        \begin{bmatrix}
        r_{11}\amp r_{12}\amp\cdots\amp r_{1n}\\
        0\amp r_{22}\amp\cdots\amp r_{2n}\\
        \vdots\amp\ddots\amp\ddots\amp\vdots\\
        0\amp\cdots\amp0\amp r_{nn}
        \end{bmatrix}.
      </me>
    </p>
    <p>
      The column equations above become the single matrix equation
      <me>
        A=QR.
      </me>
    </p>
    <p>
      The zeros below the diagonal of <m>R</m> record the order of the algorithm: column <m>j</m> uses only directions constructed by stage <m>j</m>.
    </p>

    <definition xml:id="subsec-qr-definition-3">
      <title>Reduced <m>QR</m> factorization</title>
      <statement xml:id="subsec-qr-definition-3-1">
        <p>
          Let <m>A</m> be an <m>m\times n</m> matrix with linearly independent columns. A reduced <m>QR</m> factorization of <m>A</m> is a factorization
          <me>
            A=QR,
          </me>
          where
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li><p><m>Q</m> is an <m>m\times n</m> matrix with orthonormal columns;</p></li>
          <li><p><m>R</m> is an invertible upper-triangular <m>n\times n</m> matrix.</p></li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          For the Gram–Schmidt convention used in this unit, the diagonal entries of <m>R</m> are positive:
          <me>
            r_{jj}=\|\mathbf{v}_j\|>0.
          </me>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>

    <activity xml:id="subsec-qr-definition-7">
      <title>Reading <m>Q^T Q</m></title>
      <statement xml:id="subsec-qr-definition-7-1">
        <p>
          Let
          <me>
            Q
            =
            \begin{bmatrix}
            1/\sqrt{3}\amp1/\sqrt{6}\\
            1/\sqrt{3}\amp-2/\sqrt{6}\\
            1/\sqrt{3}\amp1/\sqrt{6}
            \end{bmatrix}
            =
            \begin{bmatrix}
            \mathbf{q}_1\amp\mathbf{q}_2
            \end{bmatrix}.
          </me>
        </p>
        <ol>
          <li>
            <p>
              Compute <m>\|\mathbf{q}_1\|^2</m> and <m>\|\mathbf{q}_2\|^2</m>.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Compute <m>\mathbf{q}_1^T\mathbf{q}_2</m>.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>Compute <m>Q^TQ</m>.</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>What does the <m>(i,j)</m>-entry of <m>Q^TQ</m> measure?</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Explain why
              <me>
                Q^TQ=I_2
              </me>
              is the matrix check that the columns of <m>Q</m> are orthonormal.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </statement>
      <solution xml:id="subsec-qr-definition-7-2">
        <p>
          We have
          <me>
            \|\mathbf{q}_1\|^2
            =
            \frac13+\frac13+\frac13
            =
            1
          </me>
          and
          <me>
            \|\mathbf{q}_2\|^2
            =
            \frac16+\frac46+\frac16
            =
            1.
          </me>
        </p>
        <p>
          Also,
          <me>
            \mathbf{q}_1^T\mathbf{q}_2
            =
            \frac{1}{\sqrt{18}}
            -
            \frac{2}{\sqrt{18}}
            +
            \frac{1}{\sqrt{18}}
            =
            0.
          </me>
        </p>
        <p>
          Therefore
          <me>
            Q^TQ
            =
            \begin{bmatrix}
            \mathbf{q}_1^T\mathbf{q}_1\amp
            \mathbf{q}_1^T\mathbf{q}_2\\
            \mathbf{q}_2^T\mathbf{q}_1\amp
            \mathbf{q}_2^T\mathbf{q}_2
            \end{bmatrix}
            =
            \begin{bmatrix}
            1\amp0\\
            0\amp1
            \end{bmatrix}
            =
            I_2.
          </me>
        </p>
        <p>
          The <m>(i,j)</m>-entry of <m>Q^TQ</m> is the dot product <m>\mathbf{q}_i^T\mathbf{q}_j</m>. The diagonal entries check unit length, and the off-diagonal entries check orthogonality.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </activity>

    <theorem xml:id="subsec-qr-definition-10">
      <title>Gram–Schmidt produces <m>QR</m></title>
      <statement xml:id="subsec-qr-definition-10-1">
        <p>
          Every <m>m\times n</m> matrix <m>A</m> with linearly independent columns has a reduced <m>QR</m> factorization
          <me>
            A=QR
          </me>
          in which the columns of <m>Q</m> are orthonormal and <m>R</m> is upper triangular with positive diagonal entries.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <proof xml:id="subsec-qr-definition-10-2">
        <p>
          Apply the <xref ref="alg-u4-gram-schmidt">Gram–Schmidt algorithm</xref> to the columns
          <me>
            \mathbf{a}_1,\ldots,\mathbf{a}_n.
          </me>
        </p>
        <p>
          At stage <m>j</m>, the algorithm gives
          <me>
            \mathbf{a}_j
            =
            \sum_{i\leq j}
            r_{ij}\mathbf{q}_i.
          </me>
        </p>
        <p>
          Collecting these equations column by column gives
          <me>
            A=QR.
          </me>
        </p>
        <p>
          The columns of <m>Q</m> are orthonormal. The condition <m>i\leq j</m> makes <m>R</m> upper triangular, and
          <me>
            r_{jj}
            =
            \|\mathbf{v}_j\|
            >
            0
          </me>
          because the input columns are linearly independent.
        </p>
      </proof>
    </theorem>

    <corollary xml:id="subsec-qr-definition-13">
      <title>Square case</title>
      <statement xml:id="subsec-qr-definition-13-1">
        <p>
          Every invertible square matrix has a <m>QR</m> factorization
          <me>
            A=QR,
          </me>
          where <m>Q</m> is an orthogonal matrix and <m>R</m> is upper triangular with positive diagonal entries.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <proof>
        <p>
          An invertible square matrix has linearly independent columns. Apply <q><xref ref="subsec-qr-definition-10">Gram–Schmidt produces QR</xref>.</q> Since <m>Q</m> is square and has orthonormal columns,
          <me>
            Q^TQ=QQ^T=I.
          </me>
          Thus <m>Q</m> is an orthogonal matrix.
        </p>
      </proof>
    </corollary>

    <activity xml:id="subsec-qr-definition-11">
      <title>Build <m>Q</m> and <m>R</m> from the Gram–Schmidt ledger</title>
      <statement xml:id="subsec-qr-definition-11-1">
        <p>
          Let
          <me>
            A
            =
            \begin{bmatrix}
            3\amp1\\
            4\amp2
            \end{bmatrix}
            =
            \begin{bmatrix}
            \mathbf{a}_1\amp\mathbf{a}_2
            \end{bmatrix}.
          </me>
        </p>
        <ol>
          <li>
            <p>
              Compute
              <me>
                r_{11}=\|\mathbf{a}_1\|
              </me>
              and
              <me>
                \mathbf{q}_1
                =
                \frac{\mathbf{a}_1}{r_{11}}.
              </me>
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Compute the projection coefficient
              <me>
                r_{12}
                =
                \mathbf{q}_1^T\mathbf{a}_2.
              </me>
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Compute
              <me>
                \mathbf{p}_2
                =
                r_{12}\mathbf{q}_1
              </me>
              and
              <me>
                \mathbf{v}_2
                =
                \mathbf{a}_2-\mathbf{p}_2.
              </me>
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Compute
              <me>
                r_{22}
                =
                \|\mathbf{v}_2\|
              </me>
              and
              <me>
                \mathbf{q}_2
                =
                \frac{\mathbf{v}_2}{r_{22}}.
              </me>
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Assemble
              <me>
                Q
                =
                \begin{bmatrix}
                \mathbf{q}_1\amp\mathbf{q}_2
                \end{bmatrix}
              </me>
              and
              <me>
                R
                =
                \begin{bmatrix}
                r_{11}\amp r_{12}\\
                0\amp r_{22}
                \end{bmatrix}.
              </me>
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Check
              <me>
                Q^TQ=I_2
              </me>
              and
              <me>
                QR=A.
              </me>
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </statement>
      <solution xml:id="subsec-qr-definition-11-2">
        <p>
          The first column gives
          <me>
            r_{11}
            =
            \|\mathbf{a}_1\|
            =
            5,
            \qquad
            \mathbf{q}_1
            =
            \frac15
            \begin{bmatrix}
            3\\
            4
            \end{bmatrix}.
          </me>
        </p>
        <p>
          The projection coefficient is
          <me>
            r_{12}
            =
            \mathbf{q}_1^T\mathbf{a}_2
            =
            \frac35(1)+\frac45(2)
            =
            \frac{11}{5}.
          </me>
        </p>
        <p>
          Therefore
          <me>
            \mathbf{p}_2
            =
            r_{12}\mathbf{q}_1
            =
            \frac{11}{5}
            \begin{bmatrix}
            3/5\\
            4/5
            \end{bmatrix}
            =
            \begin{bmatrix}
            33/25\\
            44/25
            \end{bmatrix},
          </me>
          and
          <me>
            \mathbf{v}_2
            =
            \mathbf{a}_2-\mathbf{p}_2
            =
            \begin{bmatrix}
            1\\
            2
            \end{bmatrix}
            -
            \begin{bmatrix}
            33/25\\
            44/25
            \end{bmatrix}
            =
            \begin{bmatrix}
            -8/25\\
            6/25
            \end{bmatrix}.
          </me>
        </p>
        <p>
          Its length is
          <me>
            r_{22}
            =
            \|\mathbf{v}_2\|
            =
            \frac{2}{5},
          </me>
          so
          <me>
            \mathbf{q}_2
            =
            \frac{\mathbf{v}_2}{r_{22}}
            =
            \begin{bmatrix}
            -4/5\\
            3/5
            \end{bmatrix}.
          </me>
        </p>
        <p>
          Thus
          <me>
            Q
            =
            \begin{bmatrix}
            3/5\amp-4/5\\
            4/5\amp3/5
            \end{bmatrix},
            \qquad
            R
            =
            \begin{bmatrix}
            5\amp11/5\\
            0\amp2/5
            \end{bmatrix}.
          </me>
        </p>
        <p>
          The orthonormality check gives
          <me>
            Q^TQ
            =
            I_2,
          </me>
          and direct multiplication gives
          <me>
            QR
            =
            \begin{bmatrix}
            3\amp1\\
            4\amp2
            \end{bmatrix}
            =
            A.
          </me>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </activity>

    <activity xml:id="subsec-qr-definition-12">
      <title>An upper-triangular matrix already has a <m>QR</m> factorization</title>
      <statement xml:id="subsec-qr-definition-12-1">
        <p>
          Let
          <me>
            A
            =
            \begin{bmatrix}
            6\amp2\\
            0\amp3
            \end{bmatrix}.
          </me>
        </p>
        <p>
          Explain why
          <me>
            Q=I_2,
            \qquad
            R=A
          </me>
          is a <m>QR</m> factorization. Check each condition in the definition.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution xml:id="subsec-qr-definition-12-2">
        <p>
          The columns of <m>I_2</m> are orthonormal, so
          <me>
            Q^TQ=I_2.
          </me>
        </p>
        <p>
          The matrix
          <me>
            R
            =
            \begin{bmatrix}
            6\amp2\\
            0\amp3
            \end{bmatrix}
          </me>
          is upper triangular and has positive diagonal entries. Finally,
          <me>
            QR
            =
            I_2A
            =
            A.
          </me>
        </p>
        <p>
          Thus <m>A=QR</m> is a <m>QR</m> factorization.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </activity>

    <warning xml:id="warn-u4-rectangular-q">
      <title>Rectangular <m>Q</m></title>
      <p>
        In a reduced <m>QR</m> factorization of an <m>m\times n</m> matrix with <m>m\gt n</m>, the matrix <m>Q</m> has orthonormal columns, so
        <me>
          Q^TQ=I_n.
        </me>
      </p>
      <p>
        However, <m>Q</m> is not square, and generally
        <me>
          QQ^T\neq I_m.
        </me>
      </p>
      <p>
        Instead,
        <me>
          QQ^T\mathbf{b}
          =
          \operatorname{proj}_{\operatorname{col}(Q)}(\mathbf{b})
          =
          \operatorname{proj}_{\operatorname{col}(A)}(\mathbf{b}).
        </me>
      </p>
    </warning>

    <warning>
      <title>Signs in a <m>QR</m> factorization</title>
      <p>
        The positive-diagonal convention fixes the signs of the Gram–Schmidt factorization. Without that convention, one may replace a column <m>\mathbf{q}_j</m> by <m>-\mathbf{q}_j</m> and replace row <m>j</m> of <m>R</m> by its negative. The product <m>QR</m> does not change.
      </p>
      <p>
        Numerical software may therefore return <m>Q</m> and <m>R</m> with signs different from a hand calculation. Check
        <me>
          Q^TQ=I
          \qquad\text{and}\qquad
          QR=A
        </me>
        rather than requiring every entry to have the same sign.
      </p>
    </warning>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="bridge-qr-least-squares">
    <title>Least squares with <m>QR</m></title>

    <p>
      The <xref ref="thm-normal-equations-residual-orthogonality">normal equations</xref> give a direct route from a <m>QR</m> factorization to a least-squares solution.
    </p>
    <p>
      Suppose
      <me>
        A=QR
      </me>
      is a reduced <m>QR</m> factorization. The <xref ref="thm-normal-equations-residual-orthogonality">normal equations</xref> are
      <me>
        A^TA\widehat{\mathbf{x}}
        =
        A^T\mathbf{b}.
      </me>
    </p>
    <p>
      Substituting <m>A=QR</m> gives
      <me>
        R^TQ^TQR\widehat{\mathbf{x}}
        =
        R^TQ^T\mathbf{b}.
      </me>
    </p>
    <p>
      Since
      <me>
        Q^TQ=I,
      </me>
      this becomes
      <me>
        R^TR\widehat{\mathbf{x}}
        =
        R^TQ^T\mathbf{b}.
      </me>
    </p>
    <p>
      The matrix <m>R</m> is invertible, so <m>R^T</m> is invertible. Canceling <m>R^T</m> gives
      <me>
        R\widehat{\mathbf{x}}
        =
        Q^T\mathbf{b}.
      </me>
    </p>

    <algorithm xml:id="alg-u4-qr-least-squares">
      <title>Least squares with <m>QR</m></title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          <strong>Input.</strong> An <m>m\times n</m> matrix <m>A</m> with linearly independent columns and a vector <m>\mathbf{b}\in\mathbb{R}^m</m>.
        </p>
        <ol>
          <li>
            <p>
              Compute a reduced <m>QR</m> factorization
              <me>
                A=QR.
              </me>
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Compute
              <me>
                \mathbf{y}=Q^T\mathbf{b}.
              </me>
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Solve the upper-triangular system
              <me>
                R\widehat{\mathbf{x}}=\mathbf{y}
              </me>
              by back-substitution.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
        <p>
          <strong>Output.</strong> The vector <m>\widehat{\mathbf{x}}</m> is the least-squares coefficient vector. The fitted vector is
          <me>
            A\widehat{\mathbf{x}}
            =
            QR\widehat{\mathbf{x}}
            =
            QQ^T\mathbf{b}.
          </me>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </algorithm>

    <activity xml:id="subsec-qr-definition-8">
      <title>Back-substitution</title>
      <statement xml:id="subsec-qr-definition-8-1">
        <p>
          Solve
          <me>
            R\mathbf{x}
            =
            \mathbf{y},
          </me>
          where
          <me>
            R
            =
            \begin{bmatrix}
            4\amp2\amp7\\
            0\amp3\amp5\\
            0\amp0\amp2
            \end{bmatrix},
            \qquad
            \mathbf{y}
            =
            \begin{bmatrix}
            29\\
            21\\
            6
            \end{bmatrix}.
          </me>
        </p>
        <p>
          Work from the last equation upward.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution xml:id="subsec-qr-definition-8-2">
        <p>
          The last equation gives
          <me>
            2x_3=6,
            \qquad
            x_3=3.
          </me>
        </p>
        <p>
          The second equation gives
          <me>
            3x_2+5x_3=21,
          </me>
          so
          <me>
            3x_2+15=21,
            \qquad
            x_2=2.
          </me>
        </p>
        <p>
          The first equation gives
          <me>
            4x_1+2x_2+7x_3=29,
          </me>
          so
          <me>
            4x_1+4+21=29,
            \qquad
            x_1=1.
          </me>
        </p>
        <p>
          Therefore
          <me>
            \mathbf{x}
            =
            \begin{bmatrix}
            1\\
            2\\
            3
            \end{bmatrix}.
          </me>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </activity>

    <activity xml:id="act-u4-qr-code-reading">
      <title>Solving least squares with <m>QR</m> in code</title>
      <statement xml:id="act-u4-qr-code-reading-2">
        <p>
          The following code solves the regression problem from <q><xref ref="act-u4-fit-line-three-points">Fit a line to three data points</xref></q> using <m>QR</m>.
        </p>
        <program language="python"><code><![CDATA[import numpy as np

t = np.array([0.0, 1.0, 2.0])
y = np.array([1.0, 2.0, 2.0])

X = np.column_stack([np.ones_like(t), t])

Q, R = np.linalg.qr(X, mode="reduced")

c_qr = np.linalg.solve(R, Q.T @ y)
c_lstsq = np.linalg.lstsq(X, y, rcond=None)[0]

(
    Q.shape,
    R.shape,
    np.allclose(Q.T @ Q, np.eye(Q.shape[1])),
    np.allclose(Q @ R, X),
    c_qr,
    c_lstsq,
    np.allclose(c_qr, c_lstsq),
)
]]></code></program>
        <ol>
          <li>
            <p>What does the assignment</p>
            <program language="python"><code><![CDATA[Q, R = np.linalg.qr(X, mode="reduced")
]]></code></program>
            <p>store in <c>Q</c> and <c>R</c>?</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>Why does the code use <c>mode="reduced"</c>?</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>What should <c>Q.shape</c> and <c>R.shape</c> be?</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>What does</p>
            <program language="python"><code><![CDATA[Q.T @ Q
]]></code></program>
            <p>check?</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>What does</p>
            <program language="python"><code><![CDATA[Q @ R
]]></code></program>
            <p>check?</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>What array is constructed by</p>
            <program language="python"><code><![CDATA[np.eye(Q.shape[1])
]]></code></program>
            <p>in this example?</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>What mathematical vector is computed by</p>
            <program language="python"><code><![CDATA[Q.T @ y
]]></code></program>
            <p>?</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>Why does the code solve a system with <c>R</c>?</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>Why is</p>
            <program language="python"><code><![CDATA[np.linalg.solve(R, Q.T @ y)
]]></code></program>
            <p>preferable to explicitly computing an inverse of <c>R</c>?</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>Why should <c>c_qr</c> and <c>c_lstsq</c> agree?</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Does their agreement mean that
              <me>
                X\mathbf{c}=\mathbf{y}
              </me>
              has an exact solution?
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>Why may the entries of <c>Q</c> and <c>R</c> have signs different from a hand Gram–Schmidt calculation?</p>
          </li>
        </ol>
        <p><em>Tags.</em> <c>[U4-LO5, U4-LO6, U4-LO7 | C+R+T | Core]</c></p>
      </statement>
      <solution xml:id="act-u4-qr-code-reading-3">
        <p>The call</p>
        <program language="python"><code><![CDATA[Q, R = np.linalg.qr(X, mode="reduced")
]]></code></program>
        <p>
          computes a reduced <m>QR</m> factorization and stores the two returned arrays in <c>Q</c> and <c>R</c>.
        </p>
        <p>
          Since <c>X</c> has shape <c>(3, 2)</c>, reduced <m>QR</m> gives
        </p>
        <program language="python"><code><![CDATA[Q.shape == (3, 2)
R.shape == (2, 2)
]]></code></program>
        <p>The expression</p>
        <program language="python"><code><![CDATA[Q.T @ Q
]]></code></program>
        <p>
          checks whether the columns of <c>Q</c> are orthonormal. The expression
        </p>
        <program language="python"><code><![CDATA[Q @ R
]]></code></program>
        <p>
          checks whether the factors reconstruct <c>X</c>.
        </p>
        <p>Here,</p>
        <program language="python"><code><![CDATA[np.eye(Q.shape[1])
]]></code></program>
        <p>
          constructs the <m>2\times2</m> identity matrix.
        </p>
        <p>The vector</p>
        <program language="python"><code><![CDATA[Q.T @ y
]]></code></program>
        <p>
          is the right-hand side <m>Q^T\mathbf{y}</m> in the reduced system
          <me>
            R\widehat{\mathbf{c}}
            =
            Q^T\mathbf{y}.
          </me>
        </p>
        <p>
          The matrix <c>R</c> is square, invertible, and upper triangular, so the code solves this system with <c>np.linalg.solve</c>. It does not need to construct an inverse matrix.
        </p>
        <p>
          Both methods compute the same least-squares coefficient vector, so
        </p>
        <program language="python"><code><![CDATA[c_qr
]]></code></program>
        <p>and</p>
        <program language="python"><code><![CDATA[c_lstsq
]]></code></program>
        <p>
          should both be close to
        </p>
        <program language="python"><code><![CDATA[array([1.16666667, 0.5])
]]></code></program>
        <p>
          and the final <c>np.allclose</c> check should return <c>True</c>.
        </p>
        <p>
          This agreement does not mean that
          <me>
            X\mathbf{c}=\mathbf{y}
          </me>
          has an exact solution. The residual from the <xref ref="act-u4-fit-line-three-points">regression activity</xref> is nonzero.
        </p>
        <p>
          A valid <m>QR</m> factorization can use simultaneous sign changes in one column of <m>Q</m> and the corresponding row of <m>R</m>. Therefore numerical output may have different signs from a hand calculation while still satisfying
          <me>
            Q^TQ=I
            \qquad\text{and}\qquad
            QR=X.
          </me>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </activity>

    <p>
      The <xref ref="thm-normal-equations-residual-orthogonality">normal equations</xref> and <m>QR</m> compute the same least-squares solution. The normal equations expose residual orthogonality directly. <m>QR</m> reuses the orthonormal directions produced by <xref ref="alg-u4-gram-schmidt">Gram–Schmidt</xref> and reduces the computation to an upper-triangular solve.
    </p>
  </subsection>
</section>
